\newlabel{tab:stealth}{{4}{11}{Stealthy attacker on the case-study graph ($K$~carriers spread over time; $20$~seeds): as $K\!\to \!1$ the volume detector falls below random ($0.44$) while the receiving-side temporal detector holds ($0.60$), the headline operational result behind T1}{table.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Stealth vs volume breakdown}}{11}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:stealth}{{4}{11}{Stealth vs volume breakdown}{figure.4}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Baselines and the boundary of the threat model (AUC, $20$~seeds; path-based baseline in the spirit of Hopper \citep  {ho2021hopper}). (a)~Under a stealthy cascade the path-based detector wins only at $K{=}1$ and degrades with reach, while the temporal GNN is the only detector without a breakdown regime. (b)~With no propagation, a single-recipient attack is invisible to every detector ($\sim \!0.46$); the signal needs fan-out~$\ge 2$. The two blocks are not comparable at ``1'': in (a) $K{=}1$ still propagates, in (b) fan-out~$1$ is a surgical attack with no cascade at all.}}{11}{table.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:baselines}{{5}{11}{Baselines and the boundary of the threat model (AUC, $20$~seeds; path-based baseline in the spirit of Hopper \citep {ho2021hopper}). (a)~Under a stealthy cascade the path-based detector wins only at $K{=}1$ and degrades with reach, while the temporal GNN is the only detector without a breakdown regime. (b)~With no propagation, a single-recipient attack is invisible to every detector ($\sim \!0.46$); the signal needs fan-out~$\ge 2$. The two blocks are not comparable at ``1'': in (a) $K{=}1$ still propagates, in (b) fan-out~$1$ is a surgical attack with no cascade at all}{table.5}{}}
